+      behaviour other stories build against (#656, #657, #658, #659) and implements ADR 035 s1
+      (three tiers), s3 (lookup order, never blocks a play), s4 (one tool, outside Garura, find
+      and update), s5 (nothing used until a person approves a memory update) and s6 (every
+      decision names tier, file, lines). Layer 2 grounds ONLY from what is committed on main:
+      docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md (incl. s9 and the "Not decided here" list),
+      docs/adr/034-plays-answer-from-memory-inside-a-drive.md (s2, s3, s4),
+      docs/adr/016-agent-json-contract.md (the JSON-contract principle), docs/adr/011 (STM as
+      inter-skill transport; the "a play's own working files stay direct" settlement),
+      docs/adr/009, 015 and 017 as 035 amends them, docs/adr/008 (STM per issue),
+      core/grounding/glossary.md (Memory Architecture), docs/philosophy/**, and the base version
+      of .garura/project/issues/653/specs/plan.md (git show main:...). Not groundable: v1.json's
+      own description and lookup_rule prose, core/tools/memory/README.md, the new 655 plan
+      and the PR body, all of which the branch adds or rewrites. Design pointers the grounding
+      should test, taken from reading the diff, not findings: (a) eleven commands against the
+      nine the issue lists (health, pulled, decide beyond it); (b) `decide` records `by` and
+      `reply` as free text and the contract cannot tell a person from an agent, against ADR 035
+      s5 "only a person approves"; (c) the lookup treats a missing tier as `tier_missing` while
+      ADR 035 s3 says the lookup never blocks a play; (d) the README settles two questions that
+      ADR 035 lists under "Not decided here" and that contradict ADR 034 s2 as it stands on
+      main (learned checkpoint answers listed as memory), recorded only in the README and the
+      plan, not in an ADR. The README is the one path of this design content that sits in
+      another category (documentation, below); the grounding agent should read its "What is
+      not memory" and "The lookup order" sections as claims to test, never as a source.
+      No playbook covers this kind of work: a playbook is missing (follow-up task needed).
+
+  - name: executable-code
+    playbook: review/code
+    design_bearing: false   # playbook: industry best practice, not a garura-internal design; Layer 1 only
+    evidence:
+      - core/tools/memory/contract/check_contract.py   # 275 lines; stdlib-only Python 3.10+ script the runtime executes: a JSON-Schema-subset validator, answer-rule checks, and a folder boundary scan (reads files, walks the tool folder, uses ast for imports)
+    runnable_checks:
+      - command: "ruff check core/tools/memory/contract/check_contract.py"
+        note: "language linter; ruff is on this machine at /Users/kapilahuja/.local/bin/ruff. Observed read-only while assessing: All checks passed."
+      - command: "<quality-check-scoped, dispatched by the play to quality-auditor against pr.diff>"
+        note: "best-practice and severity taxonomy (CODE-*, SEC-*, DATA-*, ARCH-* rows of pr.md); run by the play, not this agent. The script only reads files and walks its own folder; no git, network or destructive operation."
+    notes: >
+      One script. The playbook's data-safety emphasis is light here (no writes outside stdout,
+      no git, no network), so the weight falls on error handling, exit codes (0, 1, 2 as its
+      docstring says) and whether the boundary scan and validator do what they claim. The
+      script is also run as the contract category's check, so its result is shared.
+
+  - name: tests
+    playbook: review/tests
+    design_bearing: false   # playbook: no design-grounding; run the suite, a red suite is a blocker
+    evidence:
+      - core/tools/memory/contract/test_check_contract.py   # 151 lines; unittest suite for check_contract.py (validator, answer rules, boundary), uses tempfile
+    runnable_checks:
+      - command: "cd core/tools/memory && python3 contract/test_check_contract.py"
+        note: "the suite. Run it, do not just read it (SE-7); a red suite is a blocker. Observed read-only while assessing (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1): Ran 20 tests, OK."
+      - command: "ruff check core/tools/memory/contract/test_check_contract.py"
+        note: "language linter on the test code. Observed: All checks passed."
+    notes: >
+      Tests the check script only, not the contract's design. The playbook's Layer 1 points
+      apply: no skipped or disabled tests, no test that asserts nothing (quality-check-scoped,
+      TEST-* rows). No test file here tests anything except check_contract.py; the samples are
+      not counted here because they are the contract's examples and the check's inputs (see the
+      first category), not test code.
+
+  - name: documentation
+    playbook: review/docs
+    design_bearing: false   # playbook: Layer 1 only, no design-grounding
+    evidence:
+      - core/tools/memory/README.md   # 141 lines; plain-words guide to the tool: tiers, lookup order, commands, request and answer rules, errors, "what is not memory", rules for the folder, how to run the check
+    notes: >
+      Human-facing prose that is not an ADR, a harness file or a memory file, so the docs
+      playbook governs: spelling, grammar, dead links, heading hierarchy, leftover TODOs, terminology
+      consistency (the tool is "Garura memory" / "Phoenix memory", never "project memory" or
+      "LTM", per ADR 035 s1), and that every claim agrees with v1.json (eleven commands, seven
+      error codes, the answer rules, the check's behaviour). The playbook names no committed prose
+      linter (a known gap), so the tool performs these checks and cites each. Judgement flag: this
+      README is not only a guide. Its "What is not memory" section settles two open design
+      questions and its "The lookup order" and "The answer rules" restate normative behaviour,
+      so it carries design content even though its category is not design-bearing. That content
+      is routed to the first category's grounding as claims to test (see its notes), not
+      handled here. Path is in this category only; it is counted once.
+
