+category: tool-interface-contract
+pr: 666
+issue: 655
+base: main
+task_id: null
+playbook: null
+playbook_missing: true   # no knowledge/review playbook covers a tool's interface contract; follow-up noted in notes
+
+# Every source below was read from the base ref (git show main:<path>) or is a read-only
+# issue/plan text that predates this branch. Nothing here is taken from v1.json's prose, the
+# README, the new 655 plan or the PR body; those were only tested as claims.
+grounded_principles:
+  - id: ADR035-S1-TIERS
+    statement: >
+      Memory has three tiers: Phoenix memory (global, the superset), Garura memory (the project's
+      long-term memory: the subset it pulled plus what the project makes itself: product model,
+      intents, decisions, the person's answers) and short-term memory (per issue, in the issue's
+      folder: plans, specs and context of the work). Agents also read the git repository, the
+      product model and the intents.
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Decision part 1 (main)"
+  - id: ADR035-S2-PULL-AND-VERSION
+    statement: >
+      A project pulls the subset it needs; a short list records what it pulled and from which
+      version of Phoenix memory. Until Griffin, Phoenix memory is a git repository and every
+      memory update is a commit, so it has history, review and versions.
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Decision part 2 (main)"
+  - id: ADR035-S3-LOOKUP-ORDER
+    statement: >
+      The tool looks in short-term memory first, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory, and
+      stops at the first tier that answers. A piece the project has not pulled yet does not
+      stop a play: the tool finds it a level up. (Kapil: "this is not blocking".)
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Decision part 3 (main)"
+  - id: ADR035-S4-ONE-TOOL
+    statement: >
+      Agents and skills never read or write memory by a path. They call one memory tool, a
+      command-line tool that both finds and updates memory, over all three tiers, so every
+      answer is cited the same way. The tool sits outside Garura; when it moves to Griffin the
+      impact on Garura and the agents is zero. It searches the way Claude Code does (find
+      files, search text, read exact lines).
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Decision part 4 (main)"
+  - id: ADR035-S5-PERSON-APPROVES
+    statement: >
+      When no tier answers, the agent raises a memory update (what is missing, a drafted entry).
+      The model or the web may help draft it. Nothing in it is used until a person approves it.
+      The approved update is written to memory first (Garura memory for what only this project
+      needs, Phoenix memory for what any project could use). Until the memory review exists a
+      person approves by typing. Holds for every agent and skill.
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Decision part 5 (main)"
+  - id: ADR035-S6-EVERY-DECISION-NAMES-MEMORY
+    statement: >
+      Every decision an agent makes names the memory it came from: the tier, the file and the
+      lines. This widens ADR 034 section 4 from a play's answers inside a drive to every decision.
+      The shape is settled in plan item 6.
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Decision part 6; docs/adr/034-plays-answer-from-memory-inside-a-drive.md, Decision part 4 (main)"
+  - id: ADR034-S4-NO-ANSWER-WITHOUT-SOURCE
+    statement: >
+      Every answer a play takes from memory is recorded with where it came from. An answer with
+      no source is not allowed.
+    source: "docs/adr/034-plays-answer-from-memory-inside-a-drive.md, Decision part 4 (main)"
+  - id: ADR035-NOT-DECIDED-HERE
+    statement: >
+      Left open by ADR 035: (1) whether ADR 034's learned checkpoint answers stay on the list of
+      memory; (2) whether files a play passes between its own steps (ADR 011; ADR 016 stm paths)
+      also go through the tool; (3) whether a drive goes on while one update waits; (4) where in
+      .garura/ Garura memory lives. The base 653 plan item 1 says (1) and (2) are "left to the
+      stories". Issue 655's text asks the story to settle (1) and (2).
+    source: "docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md, Consequences / Not decided here; .garura/project/issues/653/specs/plan.md item 1 (main); gh issue view 655 (read only)"
+  - id: ADR034-S6-CHECKPOINTS-NOT-QUESTIONS
+    statement: >
+      ADR 034 is about questions, not checkpoints. A pinned checkpoint always fires and "the
+      learned checkpoint answers do not answer it".
+    source: "docs/adr/034-plays-answer-from-memory-inside-a-drive.md, Decision part 6 (main)"
+  - id: GATECONFIG-LEDGER-HOLDS-VERDICTS
+    statement: >
+      The learned-gate ledger records, per crossing, the change shape, the predicted outcome and
+      the human action (approved_clean, approved_edited, rejected, auto_pass,
+      deferred_to_drive_review). Conditional gates auto-pass from that learned policy, so a
+      checkpoint approval can be resolved with no person answering at that moment.
+    source: "core/components/memory/standards/rules/gate-config.md, 'The three gate kinds' and 'The live-eval loop' (main)"
+  - id: ADR011-STM-PATHS-ARE-THE-CONTRACT
+    statement: >
+      Skills write structured data to STM files and hand the next step a path; the downstream
+      skill reads that path explicitly. "Paths are the contract, not data shapes." ADR 016's
+      stm.input / stm.output carry the same named paths. ADR 035 amends ADR 016 only for
+      ltm_context, resolution_trace_path and the Resolution Protocol Hook, and ADR 009 only for
+      Rule 3; ADR 011 is untouched.
+    source: "docs/adr/011-stm-as-inter-skill-data-transport.md, Rules 1-3; docs/adr/016-agent-json-contract.md; docs/adr/035 Decision part 9 (main)"
+  - id: ADR008-STM-PER-ISSUE-AND-PENDING
+    statement: >
+      STM is issue-centric, under .garura/project/issues/{issue}/. Work can start without an
+      issue (exploration); before the issue number exists, STM is written to _pending/ and moved
+      into the issue's folder in the same run.
+    source: "docs/adr/008-issue-centric-stm-and-nwwi.md, Decision parts 1, 2 and 4; core/grounding/glossary.md 'STM' and 'NWWI' rows (main)"
+  - id: ADR009-R4-FAILURE-IS-LOUD
+    statement: >
+      Failure is loud. A missing memory reference fails visibly, not silently. Rule 4 stands
+      after ADR 035 ("Rules 1, 2, 4 and 5 stand").
+    source: "docs/adr/009-skill-ltm-organizational-knowledge.md, Rules 4 (main); docs/adr/035 Decision part 9"
+  - id: ADR033-APPROVAL-IS-ABOUT-EXACT-WORDS
+    statement: >
+      Approval means a person accepted these exact words: the approved thing keeps a fingerprint
+      of its words, who approved, when, and in which review. Only the review step writes
+      approval, from a person's answers; the tool shows and records, it does not approve. (Scope
+      of ADR 033 is the product model; used here as the committed pattern ADR 035 lists as Related.)
+    source: "docs/adr/033-approval-in-the-product-model.md, Decision parts 2 and 6; ADR 035 'Related' (main)"
+  - id: PLAN653-ITEM2-SCOPE-AND-RULES
+    statement: >
+      Item 2 (the contract): commands to find memory (find, search, read, go up to Phoenix
+      memory, cite) and to update it (pull, propose, list updates, apply); what each returns and
+      its errors. "Same rules as the review tool: its own folder, a version number, no Garura
+      names inside, the engine behind it swappable." Plan-done bar: every result carries tier,
+      file, lines and fingerprint; "Missing memory is an error, never 'nothing found'". Item 5:
+      draft marked as from the person, the web or the model. Item 11: waiting updates are
+      approved, changed or rejected.
+    source: ".garura/project/issues/653/specs/plan.md items 2, 5, 11 and 'When this plan is done' (main); issue 655 text (read only)"
+  - id: REVIEWTOOL-S1-EXTRACTABLE
+    statement: >
+      An extractable tool: (1) one folder, own README, tests and version, imports no Garura code,
+      reads no Garura path; (2) Garura words are data, not code; (3) talks only through the
+      contract, a JSON schema in the tool's folder, with a test on the Garura side that checks
+      what Garura sends against it; (4) storage behind one small door; (5) settings from outside;
+      (6) everything bundled; (7) a boundary check, a test in Garura's lint, fails if any file in
+      the tool's folder mentions .garura, core/components or imports from outside its folder;
+      (8) the move is copy the folder, change one address.
+    source: ".garura/project/issues/617/specs/review-tool.md section 1 (main)"
+  - id: KBDESIGN-DURABLE-SWAPPABLE
+    statement: >
+      The search engine is disposable; the content and the contract are not. Design the contract
+      for the end state and let version 1 be a crude engine behind it; agents and skills above it
